  "findings": [
    "Correct Chrome-like lifecycle ordering invokes more YouTube callbacks (4 to 7) but no extra connected nodes or text on any of the six captured sites.",
    "Wikipedia search-input focus invokes one listener in both engines; one QuickJS-only connected node is a lazy synthetic artifact, not demonstrated page progress.",
    "Repeating DOMContentLoaded in Chrome breaks YouTube because its first handler deletes the promise resolver.",
    "The lab omits parsed initial HTML and page configuration that Chrome receives; lifecycle events do not supply that data.",
    "crates.io has a usable live Chrome page despite failed controller capture; DeepSeek returns HTTP403 in Chrome too."
  ],
  "limitations": [
    "Chrome parses full captured HTML; QuickJS directly evaluates admitted captured scripts against a synthetic DOM. Matching capture hashes verify snapshot provenance, not identical executed programs.",
    "Synthetic events are untrusted. Chrome sequential event windows include possible scheduled work; idle controls are observational. QuickJS candidates each use a fresh runtime.",
    "Raw text counts include SCRIPT/STYLE text and mean Unicode scalars, not rendered text. Chrome content-only counts are separately recorded.",
    "Frozen Chrome blocks uncaptured resources and uses synthetic content-type/CORS response headers. Live Chrome is a separate reference."
  ],
